# Test: a bare session ID clears one session, and is refused when two share it.
# QUESTION:  through the operator's `clear l2tp session id` over SSH, does a
#            unique bare session ID clear exactly its session; is a bare ID
#            that two live sessions on different tunnels share refused as
#            ambiguous, with no effect on either and the qualified clear of
#            each named; does the qualified form (tunnel-id, listener,
#            expected-id) clear exactly the one it names; and is an ended
#            session's expected-id, typed at the coordinates of the live
#            session that holds its session ID, refused with that session
#            left up?
# VALIDATES: spec-l2tp-session-identity-and-publish-stall, readable selectors
#            (command-dispatch row and the session-selector-scope functional
#            row): unique bare SID, qualified collision, ambiguity without
#            effects, stale-target survivor. Real entry points: a LAC over UDP,
#            kernel PPP, `ze cli` over SSH, a RADIUS server on the wire.
# PREVENTS:  a bare clear that picks one of two sessions sharing an ID, a
#            refusal that still tears one down, a qualified clear that ends
#            its sibling, and a stale expected-id that ends the session now
#            holding those numbers.
#
# Why not a unit test: TestHandleSessionTeardownRefusesAmbiguousSelector,
# TestL2TPSessionCaptureRefusesEqualSIDAcrossRealTunnels and the real-dispatcher
# tests in internal/component/l2tp/cmd prove the resolver over test tunnels.
# Only the daemon joins the L2TP reactor's own allocator, kernel PPP, the
# command registry over SSH, the subscriber bridge and RADIUS accounting, so
# only a .ci proves an operator reaches these answers.
#
# The collision is real, not staged. Ze draws each local session ID at random
# and keeps it unique only within its tunnel (allocateSessionID,
# internal/component/l2tp/session.go), and no config or env knob seeds it. The
# peer holds 256 calls in wait-connect on one tunnel, then places calls on a
# second tunnel one at a time, hanging up each whose ID none of the 256 has,
# until ze's allocator gives one an ID a held call has (expected after about
# 257 draws, bounded at 4096, where a miss has a chance of about 1e-7). It then
# hangs up the other held calls, and both calls become PPP sessions. A third
# PPP session on its own tunnel, opened first, is the unique one; the search
# never takes its ID.
#
# Steps, each judged on state read once after the command answered, because a
# captured teardown removes the row, publishes the session-down and sends the
# CDN before the command answers: the unique bare clear; the shared bare clear,
# refused; the qualified clear of one sharer; the other sharer's coordinates
# with the ended sharer's expected-id, refused as matching no session; the
# shared bare clear again, now unique. After each: `show l2tp sessions` lists
# exactly the live sessions with unchanged births, no live session's link saw
# a CDN or a terminate, and the RADIUS server holds one Stop with
# Acct-Terminate-Cause 6 for each cleared session and none for a live one.
# Every wait is a bounded poll on state, never a sleep.
#
# Kernel PPPoL2TP needs CAP_NET_ADMIN, so this test is needs-linux and runs in
# QEMU as root. ze.l2tp.skip-kernel-probe is deliberately not set.
#
# Ports: $PORT is the L2TP listener, $PORT2 the RADIUS mock (auth and
# accounting), $PORT3 the daemon's SSH server, which the peer's `ze cli`
# reaches through the three ze.ssh.* variables below.

stdin=config:terminator=EOF_CONF
system {
    authentication {
        # bcrypt cost-4 hash of "testpass".
        user admin {
            password "$2a$04$UlwuiuH82Unfsq.XEMPGJeDkXwbm3KW.nvVaVXOd/JeFK8VjMjrQO"
        }
    }
}
l2tp {
    enabled true
    shared-secret s3cr3t
    auth-method chap-md5
    auth {
        radius {
            nas-identifier selector-lns
            timeout 5
            retries 2
            server mock {
                address 127.0.0.1
                port $PORT2
                accounting-port $PORT2
                shared-key testing123
            }
        }
    }
    pool {
        ipv4 {
            gateway 10.99.8.1
            start 10.99.8.10
            end 10.99.8.12
        }
    }
}
environment {
    l2tp {
        server main {
            ip 127.0.0.1
            port $PORT
        }
    }
    ssh {
        enabled true
        server main {
            ip 127.0.0.1
            port $PORT3
        }
    }
}
EOF_CONF

option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin
option=env:var=TEST_SECRET:value=s3cr3t
option=env:var=ze.ssh.username:value=admin
option=env:var=ze.ssh.password:value=testpass
option=env:var=ze.ssh.port:value=$PORT3

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=le test fixture l2tp/session-selector-scope/radius $PORT2 testing123
cmd=background:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=config
# 240s bounds the peer's own waits, each a share of this budget, so a stall
# ends with the peer naming the state that never came.
cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=le test fixture l2tp/session-selector-scope $PORT:timeout=240s:exit=0
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:pattern=OK: ze gave tunnels [0-9]+ and [0-9]+ the same session ID [0-9]+ after [0-9]+ draws
expect=stdout:pattern=OK: show l2tp sessions lists session [0-9]+ on tunnels [0-9]+ and [0-9]+ with two births, and session [0-9]+ alone
expect=stdout:contains=OK: the RADIUS server holds an Accounting-Start for each of the three sessions
expect=stdout:pattern=OK: the bare unique session ID [0-9]+ cleared only its session
expect=stdout:pattern=OK: the bare shared session ID [0-9]+ was refused as ambiguous, naming both qualified clears, and both sessions remain
expect=stdout:pattern=OK: the qualified clear ended only session [0-9]+ on tunnel [0-9]+
expect=stdout:contains=OK: the ended session's expected-id was refused at the live session's coordinates, which remains
expect=stdout:pattern=OK: the bare session ID [0-9]+, unique once its sibling ended, cleared the last session
expect=stdout:contains=OK: the RADIUS server holds one Stop with Acct-Terminate-Cause 6 for each cleared session and none for a live one
